---
import Answer from "../../components/challenge/Answer.astro";
import ChallengeIntro from "../../components/challenge/ChallengeIntro.astro";
import Checklist from "../../components/challenge/Checklist.astro";
import Message from "../../components/challenge/Message.astro";
import Task from "../../components/challenge/Task.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Debug from a Log File - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="exploring-ai-capabilities" subChapterId="debug-from-log">
		<ChallengeIntro skill="You can hand the agent a log too big to read and let it follow request traces to a root cause, then write it up for the people who decide what gets fixed.">
			A policyholder couldn't start a claim and wants to know if they filed it more than once. Support forwarded the email, and
			DevOps sent the production API log for that day. The log is about 65,000 lines, zipped.
		</ChallengeIntro>

		<Message
			kind="email"
			subject="Fwd: Error starting a claim"
			from="Dana Okafor (forwarded by Support)"
			to="Web Team"
			date="Wed, 30 Sep 2026 14:47 -0400"
			attachments={[{ name: "api-prod-2026-09-30.log.zip (from DevOps)", href: "/downloads/kaiju-mutual/10.api-prod-2026-09-30.log.zip" }]}
		>
			<p>
				Hi, I tried to report damage to the noodle bar (policy BZ-118302) around 2pm today and it kept giving me an error,
				something about a row that doesn't exist. I tried a few times and then it suddenly worked. Did I file it more than once?
			</p>
		</Message>

		<Task>Write a prompt to have your agent work out what happened from the log and write a report for management.</Task>

		<Checklist
			items={[
				"Find Dana's failed and successful requests, with times in her time zone and the log's.",
				"Say whether she filed more than once.",
				"Find the root cause, backed by request IDs and log lines, and check it against the code.",
				"Find who else was affected, including anyone whose claim never went in.",
				"Say how likely it is to happen again, and how sure it is of each conclusion.",
				"Recommend a fix with a priority, in a report management can act on.",
			]}
		/>

		<Answer
			prompt={`A customer sent this email: <email>. DevOps gave me the production API log for that day: <file>. It's too big to read in one go, so search it.

Work out what happened to this customer, with times in both their time zone and the log's, and whether they filed more than once. Find the root cause, backed by request IDs and log lines, and check it against the code. Find anyone else affected, especially anyone whose claim never went in.

Write a report for management: what happened, the cause, who was affected, how likely it is to happen again, and a recommended fix with a priority. Say how sure you are of each conclusion.`}
		>
			<p>
				<code>grep error</code> finds only unrelated Tidewatch forecast failures, including one inside the window. The customer's
				error is logged at <code>warn</code>, and the email says "around 2pm" in <code>-0400</code> while the log is in UTC.
			</p>
			<p>
				The cause: at 17:50 UTC the Tidewatch sync renamed a kaiju to Cinderback. Claims point at a kaiju by name, and the claim
				form loads the kaiju list once when it opens. A form opened before the rename still offers the old name, so submitting it
				fails the foreign key check. Three customers had the form open across the rename. Dana failed three times, reopened the
				form and filed once, so there are no duplicates. PH-01844 got through by picking "I don't know". PH-02716 failed twice and
				never came back, so their damage is unreported.
			</p>
			<p>
				The failing requests don't name the customer. Tying them to one takes following each <code>requestId</code> and lining it
				up with when that customer opened the form. That link comes from timing, so a good report says how sure it is. The log also
				can't show the old kaiju name, only that the name changed.
			</p>
			<p>
				Fixes worth seeing: point claims at the kaiju by id, or treat an unknown kaiju as "don't know" and keep the claim. Also worth seeing: a clearer
				error for the customer and a follow-up call to PH-02716.
			</p>
		</Answer>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
